From: Tiago Damascena <tiagobdamascena@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 20:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] clk: rockchip: px30: keep the VOP pixel clock on the integer divider

dclk_vopb is a mux between an integer divider of cpll/npll
(dclk_vopb_src), a fractional divider hanging off that same source
(dclk_vopb_frac), and the crystal. The mux carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT
and nothing else, so every clk_set_rate() lets the core pick whichever
parent lands closest to the request. The fractional path always can:
rockchip_fractional_approximation() raises its parent to the bare PLL
(1.1 GHz) and finds an exact numerator/denominator. The integer path
only sometimes can. Which one wins therefore depends on the state the
tree is in when the request arrives.

Measured on an R36S (RK3326, ST7703-family DSI panel, 640x480 at
40.76 MHz), 6.12.91: the first modeset after boot ends on the integer
divider (cpll / 27 = 40 740 741 Hz) and the picture is right. Every
later re-enable of the CRTC -- a DPMS cycle, a system suspend at any
pm_test level, a real suspend-to-RAM -- ends on the fractional divider
(dclk_vopb_src at 1 100 000 000, dclk_vopb_frac at 40 760 000) and the
panel shows random white pixels on black, then a distorted, discoloured
picture that never recovers; the VOP logs "vblank wait timed out" on
the next modeset. The Rockchip fractional divider is a pulse-swallowing
counter: right on average, irregular per period, and fed here straight
from the PLL, above the 600 MHz ceiling the vendor kernel imposes on it
(PX30_FRAC_MAX_PRATE). The same shape made the px30 audio clocks
unusable ("clk: rockchip: limit the fractional divider input rate on
px30"). A DSI host cannot live with it as a pixel clock.

Pin the mux with CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, as dclk_vopb_src already is
for its own PLL choice. The mux stays on the parent the hardware brings
out of reset (CLKSEL_CON(5)[15:14] = 0, the integer source), and
clk_set_rate() walks down the integer divider every time -- the state
the display was proven in. vop_crtc_mode_fixup()'s rounding already
stores the rate the integer path can make, so nothing else changes.
dclk_vopl, unused on this board, is left as it is.

Signed-off-by: Tiago Damascena <tiagobdamascena@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-px30.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-px30.c
@@ -255,8 +255,23 @@
 	MUX(0, "clk_uart5_mux", mux_uart5_p, CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT,
 			PX30_CLKSEL_CON(47), 14, 2, MFLAGS);
 
+/*
+ * The VOP pixel clock never leaves the integer divider. The fractional
+ * divider path (dclk_vopb_frac) is a pulse-swallowing counter: it hits the
+ * requested rate on average, with an irregular period, and on this SoC it
+ * is fed straight from the PLL (1.1 GHz) -- the same shape that made the
+ * px30 audio clocks unusable (see rockchip_fractional_approximation's
+ * max_prate). A DSI panel fed that way shows random white pixels, then a
+ * distorted, discoloured picture, and the VOP's vblank stops arriving on
+ * time. Without CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT the mux is free to pick the frac
+ * whenever it lands closer to the request than the integer divider does,
+ * and it does so on every re-enable of the CRTC (DPMS, system suspend),
+ * while the first modeset after boot happened to choose the integer path:
+ * the display worked until the first time it was turned off and on.
+ */
 static struct rockchip_clk_branch px30_dclk_vopb_fracmux __initdata =
-	MUX(0, "dclk_vopb_mux", mux_dclk_vopb_p, CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT,
+	MUX(0, "dclk_vopb_mux", mux_dclk_vopb_p,
+			CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT | CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT,
 			PX30_CLKSEL_CON(5), 14, 2, MFLAGS);
 
 static struct rockchip_clk_branch px30_dclk_vopl_fracmux __initdata =
